title: Kagome
position: -276,-22
---
<<set $name3 to "Ghost Boy">>
<<set $mean2 to 0>>
//you enter what looks like a school yard. It seems to be a corner and it's empty save for one child sitting on the ground by himself.
//There's a sign here as well, next to the door (which now looks like a fence gate) It reads:
Task 2 - Play with the child.
//You approach the boy. As you get closer, this text appears:
You can hear faint muttering, sounding like poorly contained sobs.
The boy doesn't look very happy.
//then when you interact with him:
{$name3}: Everyone's always being so mean to me.
{$name3}: I bet you just came to make fun of me as well.
-> Of course not! Only losers make fun of others.
    The little boy looks somewhat skeptical.
    {$name3}: Oh really? You gotta prove you mean it.
    {$name1}: No problem. How about a little game?
-> ...well, you do look a little thick.
    <<set $mean2 = $mean2 + 1>>
    The boy's expression is stuck somewhere between indignation and utter confusion, rendering it almost blank.
    For a second, you're afraid that being needlessly mean to the poor ghost kid would turn out to be the last thing you ever do.
    But instead ...
    {$name3}: WAHHHHHHH! *sob sob*
    Great. Was that worth it?
    -> Absolutely.
        Well, you've still got to finish your task, so you better calm the brat down.
        <<set $mean2 = $mean2 + 1>>
    -> Actually, I feel kinda bad now.
        There may be hope for you yet. Then you better give him a decent apology now.
    {$name1}: Look, kiddo, I'm sorry. I didn't mean it like that. Are you up for a little game, maybe?
{$name3}: ... a game?
{$name1}: Yeah. Is there any game you like in particular?
{$name3}: I hate them all. I always lose. Doesn't matter which game it is.
-> That's hardly surprising. Ahem, nooo ... I mean whaaat ...
    <<set $mean2 = $mean2 + 1>>
    {$name3}: Ugh, I knew it, you're just like everybody else! Meanie, meanie!!
    {$name3}: I'll show you! I can totally beat you!!
    {$name1}: Bring it on, loser.
    Wow. You're an asshole. But somehow, the kid seems motivated now.
-> Winning's not everything!
    {$name3}: Easy for you to say. I'm sick of always losing! Always, always. I hate it. It's no fun!
    {$name1}: Of course, I get that. Sorry. I'm sure you'll win this time!
{$name1}: So, what game are we playing?
{$name3}: ... do you know "Kagome Kagome"?
-> Yes.
    {$name3}: Ah, I don't have to explain the rules then.
    {$name3}: Then you know how much it stinks, always having to be Oni!
-> No.
    {$name3}: Really?? We play it all the time here! It's pretty simple.
    <<detour Rules>>
{$name3}: I know the chant by heart, because I hear it all the time. But I never get to sing it. Because I can never guess who's standing behind me.
{$name3}: Everyone always makes me be "Oni" first. I know they're cheating, but I can't figure out how.
{$name3}: It stinks, it stinks!
{$name3}: Just one time, I don't want to be "Oni". Just one time!
{$name1}: Well, I'm pretty bad at games myself, so now's your chance! I promise I won't cheat.
{$name3}: ... pinky promise?
{$name1}: Pinky promise.
{$name3}: Okay. Deal.
{$name3}: So, do you remember all the rules?
-> Yup.
    {$name3}: Okay. No excuses then!
-> Can you explain one more time?
    {$name3}: *sigh* Okay, but just once.
    <<detour Rules>>
{$name3}: Let's get started then. You ... you get behind me first.
{$name1}: Huh? Don't you want to chant first? You don't have to be "Oni".
{$name3}: ... I can win. I'll win as "Oni" first.
The boy covers his eyes with his hands.
As you get into position, you notice shadowy, faceless figures appear next to you. The two on either side of you are holding out their hands towards you.
-> Take their hands.
    Despite being transparent, the hands are solid.
    And they are incredibly cold. So much so that you instinctively try to pull back.
    But you can't. Your hands are interlocked with the shadow figures like they are an extension of your own body.
    You feel a sense of cold floating up your arms and into your body. You're part of their merry circle now.
-> Don't take them.
    The faceless things all collectively turn to look at you, or at least you imagine they do, as it's hard to tell when they don't have any distinguishing features to tell their face from the rest of their head.
    Now what?
    The boy starts muttering impatiently.
    What are you going to do now?
    -> take their hands.
        Despite being transparent, the hands are solid.
        And they are incredibly cold. So much so that you instinctively try to pull back. 
        But you can't. Your hands are interlocked with the shadow figures like they are an extension of your own body.
        You feel a sense of cold floating up your arms and into your body. You're part of their merry circle now.
    -> take their hands.
        Despite being transparent, the hands are solid.
        And they are incredibly cold. So much so that you instinctively try to pull back.
        But you can't. Your hands are interlocked with the shadow figures like they are an extension of your own body.
        You feel a sense of cold floating up your arms and into your body. You're part of their merry circle now.

//the game mechanics are as follows: you play two rounds. The first one, you have to decide for each round whether to move forward or stand still for every verse of the chant. If you stand still more than once in succession, though, the boy will notice and identify you as a cheater which will result in a game over.
//Round two, you're "Oni". That means you have to guess either "Nobody" or "You" (maybe I'll give the boy a name that'd make sense). You have to determine if it's the boy standing behind you by how his voice sounds. It'll be loudest when he's right behind you.

// what happens when you're too mean to Ghost Boy? Maybe actually nothing. Maybe there's no consequences for that until you meet the Drowned Child later.

<<jump Cafeteria>>

